Chapter: T3ISA Emitter

 Lowers the Codex typed IR to T3ISA assembly text, the balanced-ternary
 27-trit machine defined by the external maniT project.

 T3ISA is designed and specified by Manish Jagdish Thatte. This emitter is
 an independent implementation written from the published specification
 alone, docs/t3isa-reference.md at tag t3isa-spec-v1.3 in
 https://github.com/manishthatte/maniTC, and from no other part of that
 project. Their emulator is used as a black-box oracle over its published
 command line; their backend is not read.

 The assembly form, the register roles and the syscall numbers are that
 project's specification, not ours: arguments arrive in R1 through R8, a
 single return value leaves in R1, R26 is the stack pointer growing down
 from 60,000 in a 65,536 word address space, R0 always reads zero, and
 every register is caller-save. Syscall 1 prints an integer, 3 a string
 and 4 a newline. Strings are not addressable memory on this machine; a
 string literal is an entry in a sidecar table and its "address" is an
 index into that table, which is why text is emitted into a .data section
 rather than laid out in memory.

 The overflow story is the one place the target is kinder than the source.
 T3ISA arithmetic SATURATES at the 27-trit boundary, so OvClamping needs no
 lowering at all, and OvWrapping has no meaning here because wrapping is
 defined by a binary modulus. That is refused rather than approximated.

Section: Emitter Configuration

 A word holds at most 3,812,798,742,493, so a Codex integer declared wider
 than that does not fit and is refused rather than silently truncated. The
 immediate field of a standard instruction is three trits, so any constant
 outside plus or minus 13 has to be materialised with TLIT into a scratch
 register first; t3-sp-adjust and the slot helpers below are the only
 places that matters.

  t3-word-max : Integer
  t3-word-max = 3812798742493

  t3-imm-max : Integer
  t3-imm-max = 13

  t3-max-args : Integer
  t3-max-args = 8

  t3-scratch-a : Text
  t3-scratch-a = "R9"

  t3-scratch-b : Text
  t3-scratch-b = "R10"

  t3-scratch-addr : Text
  t3-scratch-addr = "R21"

 The address space is 65,536 words, and spec v1.3 section 3 gives the
 reference emulator's layout within it: the stack starts at 60,000 and grows
 down, module globals sit at 61,000, emulator scratch at 62,000, and their
 own allocator runs up from 63,000 to the top. Only the address space is
 architectural; the rest is that emulator's arrangement, so the free run an
 aggregate can live in is what lies below the stack's start and above the
 string pseudo-addresses at code_size + 1024. The base, the ceiling and the
 cell holding the bump pointer sit inside that run: 32,768 is far above the
 string addresses, and 49,152 leaves 10,848 words of stack below 60,000.
 MEASURED 2026-08-10 rather than assumed: words at 32,768, 32,771, 40,000
 and 49,151 all store and load back, through the direct and the offset form
 alike.

 The earlier revision of the reference gave the initial stack pointer as
 65,535 and their allocator base as "about 50,000", and this chapter was
 built on those numbers. Both were corrected in v1.3. The region chosen here
 is clear under either reading, which is why the correction moved no code.

  t3-heap-base : Integer
  t3-heap-base = 32768

  t3-heap-ceiling : Integer
  t3-heap-ceiling = 49152

  t3-heap-cell : Integer
  t3-heap-cell = 32767

Section: Context

  T3Slot = record {
   name : Text,
   index : Integer
  }

  T3Arity = record {
   fn-name : Text,
   fn-arity : Integer
  }

  T3Ctx = record {
   env : List T3Slot,
   next-slot : Integer,
   sp-delta : Integer,
   label-n : Integer,
   strings : List Text,
   arities : List T3Arity,
   refusals : List Text,
   fn-tag : Text,
   type-defs : List ATypeDef
  }

  T3Res = record {
   code : Text,
   ctx : T3Ctx
  }

  t3-empty-ctx : List T3Arity, List ATypeDef -> T3Ctx
  t3-empty-ctx (ars) (tds) =
   T3Ctx {
    env = [],
    next-slot = 0,
    sp-delta = 0,
    label-n = 0,
    strings = [],
    arities = ars,
    refusals = [],
    fn-tag = "",
    type-defs = tds
   }

 Every expression emitter leaves exactly one word on the stack, so sp-delta
 moves by a statically known amount at every point and a local's address is
 always sp-delta plus its frame index. That invariant is what lets a pure
 recursive walk address locals correctly under arbitrary nesting; break it
 in one arm and every load in the enclosing function reads the wrong word.

  t3-push-delta : T3Ctx -> T3Ctx
  t3-push-delta (c) =
   T3Ctx { env = c.env, next-slot = c.next-slot, sp-delta = c.sp-delta + 1,
           label-n = c.label-n, strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities,
           refusals = c.refusals, fn-tag = c.fn-tag, type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-pop-delta : T3Ctx -> T3Ctx
  t3-pop-delta (c) =
   T3Ctx { env = c.env, next-slot = c.next-slot, sp-delta = c.sp-delta - 1,
           label-n = c.label-n, strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities,
           refusals = c.refusals, fn-tag = c.fn-tag, type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-bump-label : T3Ctx -> T3Ctx
  t3-bump-label (c) =
   T3Ctx { env = c.env, next-slot = c.next-slot, sp-delta = c.sp-delta,
           label-n = c.label-n + 1, strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities,
           refusals = c.refusals, fn-tag = c.fn-tag, type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-bind-slot : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Ctx
  t3-bind-slot (c) (n) =
   T3Ctx { env = list-push (c.env) (T3Slot { name = n, index = c.next-slot }),
           next-slot = c.next-slot + 1, sp-delta = c.sp-delta,
           label-n = c.label-n, strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities,
           refusals = c.refusals, fn-tag = c.fn-tag, type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-add-string : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Ctx
  t3-add-string (c) (s) =
   T3Ctx { env = c.env, next-slot = c.next-slot, sp-delta = c.sp-delta,
           label-n = c.label-n, strings = list-push (c.strings) s,
           arities = c.arities, refusals = c.refusals, fn-tag = c.fn-tag,
           type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-refuse-ctx : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Ctx
  t3-refuse-ctx (c) (why) =
   T3Ctx { env = c.env, next-slot = c.next-slot, sp-delta = c.sp-delta,
           label-n = c.label-n, strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities,
           refusals = list-push (c.refusals) why, fn-tag = c.fn-tag,
           type-defs = c.type-defs }

  t3-reset-fn : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Ctx
  t3-reset-fn (c) (tag) =
   T3Ctx { env = [], next-slot = 0, sp-delta = 0, label-n = c.label-n,
           strings = c.strings, arities = c.arities, refusals = c.refusals,
           fn-tag = tag, type-defs = c.type-defs }

Section: Environment Lookup

  t3-slot-of : List T3Slot, Text -> Integer
  t3-slot-of (env) (n) = t3-slot-of-loop env n (list-length env - 1)

 Scanned newest-first so an inner let shadows an outer binding of the same
 name, which is what the source language means and what the IR preserves.

  t3-slot-of-loop : List T3Slot, Text, Integer -> Integer
  t3-slot-of-loop (env) (n) (i) =
   if i < 0 then -1
   else let s = list-at env i
   in if s.name == n then s.index
   else t3-slot-of-loop env n (i - 1)

  t3-arity-of : List T3Arity, Text -> Integer
  t3-arity-of (ars) (n) = t3-arity-of-loop ars n 0

  t3-arity-of-loop : List T3Arity, Text, Integer -> Integer
  t3-arity-of-loop (ars) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ars then -1
   else let a = list-at ars i
   in if a.fn-name == n then a.fn-arity
   else t3-arity-of-loop ars n (i + 1)

Section: Name Sanitization

 T3ISA label names allow alphanumerics, underscore, dot and the qualified
 marker. Codex names are kebab-case, so the hyphens become underscores.

  t3-sanitize : Text -> Text
  t3-sanitize (n) = text-replace n "-" "_"

Section: Instruction Helpers

  t3-line : Text -> Text
  t3-line (s) = "    " & s & "\n"

  t3-label-line : Text -> Text
  t3-label-line (l) = "  " & l & ":\n"

  t3-fresh-label : T3Ctx -> Text
  t3-fresh-label (c) = c.fn-tag & "_L" & show (c.label-n)

 A stack push is two instructions and it is the only thing that moves the
 stack pointer during a body, so sp-delta and this pair must stay in step.

  t3-push : Text -> Text
  t3-push (r) = t3-line "TSUB  R26, R26, #1" & t3-line ("STORE " & r & ", [R26+#0]")

  t3-pop : Text -> Text
  t3-pop (r) = t3-line ("LOAD  " & r & ", [R26+#0]") & t3-line "TADD  R26, R26, #1"

 The immediate field is three trits. Anything wider has to go through TLIT
 into a scratch register, which is why every stack and slot access below
 asks whether its offset fits before choosing a form.

  t3-fits-imm : Integer -> Boolean
  t3-fits-imm (k) = if k > t3-imm-max then False else if k < 0 - t3-imm-max then False else True

  t3-sp-adjust : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-sp-adjust (op) (n) =
   if n == 0 then ""
   else if t3-fits-imm n then t3-line (op & "  R26, R26, #" & show n)
   else t3-line ("TLIT  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", #" & show n) & t3-line (op & "  R26, R26, " & t3-scratch-addr)

  t3-load-slot : T3Ctx, Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-load-slot (c) (r) (slot) =
   let off = c.sp-delta + slot
   in if t3-fits-imm off then t3-line ("LOAD  " & r & ", [R26+#" & show off & "]")
   else t3-load-slot-far r off

  t3-load-slot-far : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-load-slot-far (r) (off) =
   t3-line ("TLIT  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", #" & show off)
   & t3-line ("TADD  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", R26, " & t3-scratch-addr)
   & t3-line ("LOAD  " & r & ", [" & t3-scratch-addr & "+#0]")

  t3-store-slot : T3Ctx, Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-store-slot (c) (r) (slot) =
   let off = c.sp-delta + slot
   in if t3-fits-imm off then t3-line ("STORE " & r & ", [R26+#" & show off & "]")
   else t3-store-slot-far r off

  t3-store-slot-far : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-store-slot-far (r) (off) =
   t3-line ("TLIT  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", #" & show off)
   & t3-line ("TADD  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", R26, " & t3-scratch-addr)
   & t3-line ("STORE " & r & ", [" & t3-scratch-addr & "+#0]")

Section: Allocation

 A bump allocator in a fixed memory cell. There is no register to keep it in:
 every register on this machine is caller-save, so a pointer held across a
 call would be lost, and the emulator's own allocator is reached only through
 syscalls that hand back opaque handles rather than addresses a LOAD can use.
 The cell is the only representation that survives a call.

 R22 carries the object address out of this sequence and every field store
 below reads it, so nothing between the allocation and the last store may
 write R22.

  t3-alloc-code : Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-alloc-code (n) (lbl) =
   t3-const-into "R21" t3-heap-cell
   & t3-line "LOAD  R22, [R21+#0]"
   & t3-const-into "R23" n
   & t3-line "TADD  R24, R22, R23"
   & t3-const-into "R25" t3-heap-ceiling
   & t3-line "TCMP  R23, R24, R25"
   & t3-line ("TBRANCH R23, " & lbl & "_hbad, " & lbl & "_hok, " & lbl & "_hok")
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_hbad")
   & t3-line "TLIT  R1, #1"
   & t3-line "TDIV  R1, R1, R0"
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_hok")
   & t3-const-into "R21" t3-heap-cell
   & t3-line "STORE R24, [R21+#0]"

 Exhaustion traps rather than running on. The heap grows toward the stack, so
 an unguarded bump would overwrite live frames and answer with corrupted data
 instead of failing, which is the shape of wrongness this backend exists to
 refuse. Division by zero is the fault used because it is the only one this
 machine is measured to raise.

 The cell address is re-materialised before the store rather than assumed to
 have survived in R21, because a wide constant would have used R21 as its own
 scratch. Two words to remove an invariant nothing would have checked.

  t3-heap-init : Text
  t3-heap-init =
   t3-const-into "R21" t3-heap-cell
   & t3-const-into "R22" t3-heap-base
   & t3-line "STORE R22, [R21+#0]"

 An object field is at a small offset, but the immediate is only three trits,
 so a record wider than thirteen fields needs the address computed. R23 is the
 scratch because R22 holds the object.

  t3-store-obj : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-store-obj (r) (k) =
   if t3-fits-imm k then t3-line ("STORE " & r & ", [R22+#" & show k & "]")
   else t3-const-into "R23" k & t3-line "TADD  R23, R22, R23" & t3-line ("STORE " & r & ", [R23+#0]")

  t3-load-obj : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-load-obj (r) (k) =
   if t3-fits-imm k then t3-line ("LOAD  " & r & ", [R22+#" & show k & "]")
   else t3-const-into "R23" k & t3-line "TADD  R23, R22, R23" & t3-line ("LOAD  " & r & ", [R23+#0]")

Section: Aggregate Layout

 A record is its fields in DECLARED order, one word each. A variant is a tag
 word followed by the constructor's fields, the tag being the constructor's
 position in the type's own list. Both indices come from the type definitions
 the IR carries beside the chapter, never from the order a literal happened to
 write its fields in, so a record built with its fields out of order reads back
 correctly.

  t3-find-field : List RecordField, Text, Integer -> Integer
  t3-find-field (fs) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fs then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at fs i).name.value == n then i
   else t3-find-field fs n (i + 1)

  t3-find-ctor : List SumCtor, Text, Integer -> Integer
  t3-find-ctor (cs) (n) (i) =
   if i >= list-length cs then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at cs i).name.value == n then i
   else t3-find-ctor cs n (i + 1)

  t3-type-name : CodexType -> Text
  t3-type-name (ty) =
   when ty
    is RecordTy (n) (a) (f) -> n.value
    is SumTy (n) (a) (c) -> n.value
    is ConstructedTy (n) (a) -> n.value
    is EffectfulTy (es) (ss) (inner) -> t3-type-name inner
    is otherwise -> ""

 A type carries its own fields when it is a RecordTy and only names them when
 it is a ConstructedTy, so both roads are tried before the field is given up
 on.

  t3-record-fields : List ATypeDef, CodexType -> List RecordField
  t3-record-fields (tds) (ty) =
   when ty
    is RecordTy (n) (a) (fs) -> if list-length fs > 0 then fs else record-fields-by-name tds (n.value) 0
    is otherwise -> record-fields-by-name tds (t3-type-name ty) 0

  t3-sum-ctors : List ATypeDef, CodexType -> List SumCtor
  t3-sum-ctors (tds) (ty) =
   when ty
    is SumTy (n) (a) (cs) -> if list-length cs > 0 then cs else sum-ctors-by-name tds (n.value) 0
    is otherwise -> sum-ctors-by-name tds (t3-type-name ty) 0

  t3-field-index : List ATypeDef, CodexType, Text -> Integer
  t3-field-index (tds) (ty) (fname) = t3-find-field (t3-record-fields tds ty) fname 0

  t3-ctor-index : List ATypeDef, CodexType, Text -> Integer
  t3-ctor-index (tds) (ty) (name) = t3-find-ctor (t3-sum-ctors tds ty) name 0

 The declared type of a field or of a constructor's argument, which is where a
 narrow in that position finds its band. The design's own table names three
 positions a band can be held in and this is the third of them.

  t3-field-type : List ATypeDef, CodexType, Text -> CodexType
  t3-field-type (tds) (ty) (fname) =
   let fs = t3-record-fields tds ty
   in let i = t3-find-field fs fname 0
   in if i < 0 then ErrorTy else (list-at fs i).type-val

  t3-ctor-field-type : List ATypeDef, CodexType, Text, Integer -> CodexType
  t3-ctor-field-type (tds) (ty) (name) (i) =
   let cs = t3-sum-ctors tds ty
   in let ci = t3-find-ctor cs name 0
   in if ci < 0 then ErrorTy
   else let flds = (list-at cs ci).fields
   in if i >= list-length flds then ErrorTy else list-at flds i

 The type of a subexpression, needed only so a field access can name the
 record it is reaching into. A field-access node carries the FIELD's type, not
 the record's, so the record type has to come from the receiver.

  t3-expr-type : IRExpr -> CodexType
  t3-expr-type (e) =
   when e
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrIf (cnd) (th) (el) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrMatch (s) (bs) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is IrLet (n) (ty) (v) (b) (sp) -> t3-expr-type b
    is otherwise -> ErrorTy

Section: Record Construction

 Fields are evaluated in the order written, onto the stack, and only then is
 the object allocated and the values popped back into their declared slots.
 Allocating first would leave the object pointer live across the evaluation of
 every remaining field, and a nested construction inside one of them would
 take R22 away.

  t3-emit-record : T3Ctx, List IRFieldVal, CodexType, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-record (c) (fs) (ty) (depth) =
   let order = t3-field-order (c.type-defs) ty fs 0 []
   in if t3-has-missing order 0 then t3-refuse c ("record whose field layout is not recoverable: " & t3-type-name ty)
   else t3-emit-record-go c fs ty order depth

  t3-field-order : List ATypeDef, CodexType, List IRFieldVal, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  t3-field-order (tds) (ty) (fs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length fs then acc
   else t3-field-order tds ty fs (i + 1) (list-push acc (t3-field-index tds ty ((list-at fs i).name)))

  t3-has-missing : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  t3-has-missing (xs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i < 0 then True
   else t3-has-missing xs (i + 1)

  t3-emit-record-go : T3Ctx, List IRFieldVal, CodexType, List Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-record-go (c) (fs) (ty) (order) (depth) =
   let n = list-length fs
   in let ev = t3-emit-field-vals c fs ty 0 depth ""
   in let c1 = t3-drop-deltas (ev.ctx) n
   in let lbl = t3-fresh-label c1
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta (t3-bump-label c1)
   in T3Res { code = ev.code & t3-alloc-code n lbl & t3-store-fields order (n - 1) "" & t3-push "R22", ctx = c2 }

  t3-emit-field-vals : T3Ctx, List IRFieldVal, CodexType, Integer, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-field-vals (c) (fs) (ty) (i) (depth) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length fs then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else let fv = list-at fs i
   in let want = t3-field-type (c.type-defs) ty (fv.name)
   in let s = t3-emit-expr-expecting c (fv.value) (depth + 1) want
   in t3-emit-field-vals (s.ctx) fs ty (i + 1) depth (acc & s.code)

 Popped in reverse, because the last value written is the one on top.

  t3-store-fields : List Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-store-fields (order) (i) (acc) =
   if i < 0 then acc
   else t3-store-fields order (i - 1) (acc & t3-pop "R9" & t3-store-obj "R9" (list-at order i))

Section: Variant Construction

  t3-emit-ctor : T3Ctx, Text, CodexType, Integer, List IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-ctor (c) (name) (ty) (tag) (args) (depth) =
   let n = list-length args
   in let ev = t3-emit-ctor-args c args name ty 0 depth ""
   in let c1 = t3-drop-deltas (ev.ctx) n
   in let lbl = t3-fresh-label c1
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta (t3-bump-label c1)
   in let tagw = t3-const-into "R9" tag & t3-store-obj "R9" 0
   in let body = ev.code & t3-alloc-code (n + 1) lbl & tagw & t3-store-ctor-fields (n - 1) ""
   in T3Res { code = body & t3-push "R22", ctx = c2 }

  t3-emit-ctor-args : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Text, CodexType, Integer, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-ctor-args (c) (args) (name) (ty) (i) (depth) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length args then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else let want = t3-ctor-field-type (c.type-defs) ty name i
   in let s = t3-emit-expr-expecting c (list-at args i) (depth + 1) want
   in t3-emit-ctor-args (s.ctx) args name ty (i + 1) depth (acc & s.code)

  t3-store-ctor-fields : Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-store-ctor-fields (i) (acc) =
   if i < 0 then acc
   else t3-store-ctor-fields (i - 1) (acc & t3-pop "R9" & t3-store-obj "R9" (i + 1))

Section: Field Access

 THE IR ALREADY CARRIES THE RESOLVED FIELD INDEX, AND ONLY AT THE ACCESS.
 A field-access node names its field `px/0`, name and declared index joined
 by a slash, while the field-val nodes of a record literal name theirs plainly
 as `px`. So the two halves of the same record are described differently: the
 reader is handed the answer and the writer is not. Measured 2026-08-10 on the
 IR text for this plug's own aggregates program.

 The suffix is used when it is there, because it is the compiler's own
 resolution and cannot drift from it. The lookup through the type definitions
 is kept and CROSS-CHECKED against it rather than being merely a fallback: the
 two roads to a field's offset should never disagree, so a disagreement is a
 refusal and not a preference. It costs one comparison and cannot fire on a
 well-formed chapter.

  t3-field-base-name : Text -> Text
  t3-field-base-name (n) = list-at (text-split n "/") 0

  t3-field-slash-index : Text -> Integer
  t3-field-slash-index (n) =
   let parts = text-split n "/"
   in if list-length parts < 2 then 0 - 1
   else text-to-integer (list-at parts (list-length parts - 1))

  t3-emit-field-access : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Text, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-field-access (c) (rec) (fname) (depth) =
   let direct = t3-field-slash-index fname
   in let looked = t3-field-index (c.type-defs) (t3-expr-type rec) (t3-field-base-name fname)
   in if direct >= 0 & looked >= 0 & direct /= looked
    then t3-refuse c ("field ." & fname & " resolves to offset " & show direct & " by name and " & show looked & " by type definition")
   else t3-emit-field-access-at c rec (if direct >= 0 then direct else looked) fname depth

  t3-emit-field-access-at : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer, Text, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-field-access-at (c) (rec) (idx) (fname) (depth) =
   if idx < 0 then t3-refuse c ("field access whose record type is not recoverable from the receiver: ." & fname)
   else t3-emit-field-access-go c rec idx depth

  t3-emit-field-access-go : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-field-access-go (c) (rec) (idx) (depth) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c rec (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c1
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R22" & t3-load-obj "R9" idx & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

Section: Refusal

 A refusal is a line in the output AND an entry in the context, so a
 malformed program cannot produce an artifact that merely looks short. The
 runner greps for the marker and fails; emitting something plausible for a
 construct this backend cannot express is the one outcome that would make
 the whole experiment worthless.

  t3-refuse : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Res
  t3-refuse (c) (why) =
   let c2 = t3-refuse-ctx c why
   in let c3 = t3-push-delta c2
   in T3Res { code = t3-line ("; !UNSUPPORTED: " & why) & t3-line "TLIT  R9, #0" & t3-push "R9", ctx = c3 }

 The statement-context refusal leaves the stack alone. An expression
 refusal must still push, because every expression arm pushes and an
 unbalanced one would corrupt every frame offset above it.

  t3-refuse-void : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Res
  t3-refuse-void (c) (why) =
   T3Res { code = t3-line ("; !UNSUPPORTED: " & why), ctx = t3-refuse-ctx c why }

Section: Integer Range Check

 A Codex integer band wider than a 27-trit word cannot be represented, and
 the failure would be a wrong answer rather than a crash, so it is refused
 at the type rather than at the value.

  t3-int-ty-fits : CodexType -> Boolean
  t3-int-ty-fits (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (ov) -> if hi > t3-word-max then False else if lo < 0 - t3-word-max then False else True
    is otherwise -> True

  t3-ov-supported : CodexType -> Boolean
  t3-ov-supported (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (ov) -> t3-ov-mode-ok ov
    is otherwise -> True

  t3-ov-mode-ok : OverflowMode -> Boolean
  t3-ov-mode-ok (m) =
   when m
    is OvWrapping -> False
    is otherwise -> True

Section: Binary Operators

 TADD, TSUB, TMUL, TDIV and TMOD are the machine's own arithmetic and need
 no lowering. Comparison is the interesting case: TCMP writes the SIGN of
 the difference, minus one, zero or plus one, into a register, and a Codex
 boolean is zero or one. Each relation is therefore a small arithmetic
 recovery from that sign rather than a branch, which is why TMIN, TMAX and
 TNEG appear here at all.

  t3-arith-mnemonic : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  t3-arith-mnemonic (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt -> "TADD"
    is IrSubInt -> "TSUB"
    is IrMulInt -> "TMUL"
    is IrDivInt -> "TDIV"
    is IrRemInt -> "TMOD"
    is IrAnd -> "TMIN"
    is IrOr -> "TMAX"
    is otherwise -> ""

  t3-is-comparison : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  t3-is-comparison (op) =
   when op
    is IrEq -> True
    is IrNotEq -> True
    is IrLt -> True
    is IrGt -> True
    is IrLtEq -> True
    is IrGtEq -> True
    is otherwise -> False

 Given s = sign(l - r) in the scratch register, gt is max(s, 0) and lt is
 the negation of min(s, 0). Everything else is one subtraction away.

  t3-cmp-recover : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  t3-cmp-recover (op) =
   when op
    is IrGt -> t3-cmp-gt
    is IrLtEq -> t3-cmp-gt & t3-cmp-invert
    is IrLt -> t3-cmp-lt
    is IrGtEq -> t3-cmp-lt & t3-cmp-invert
    is IrNotEq -> t3-cmp-ne
    is IrEq -> t3-cmp-ne & t3-cmp-invert
    is otherwise -> ""

  t3-cmp-gt : Text
  t3-cmp-gt = t3-line "TMAX  R9, R9, R0"

  t3-cmp-lt : Text
  t3-cmp-lt = t3-line "TMIN  R9, R9, R0" & t3-line "TNEG  R9, R9"

 Nonzero either way is not-equal: max(s,0) plus negated min(s,0) is one
 exactly when the sign is nonzero, and zero when the operands are equal.

  t3-cmp-ne : Text
  t3-cmp-ne =
   t3-line "TMAX  R10, R9, R0"
   & t3-line "TMIN  R9, R9, R0"
   & t3-line "TNEG  R9, R9"
   & t3-line "TADD  R9, R9, R10"

  t3-cmp-invert : Text
  t3-cmp-invert = t3-line "TLIT  R10, #1" & t3-line "TSUB  R9, R10, R9"

Section: Expression Emission

 Every arm leaves exactly one word on the stack. Depth is fuelled because
 a malformed IR tree would otherwise recurse without bound on a machine
 with no stack guard.

  t3-emit-expr : T3Ctx, IRExpr -> T3Res
  t3-emit-expr (c) (e) = t3-emit-expr-at c e 0

  t3-emit-expr-at : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-expr-at (c) (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= 256 then t3-refuse c "expression nesting beyond 256"
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> t3-emit-int-lit c n
    is IrBoolLit (b) (sp) -> t3-emit-const c (if b then 1 else 0)
    is IrCharLit (n) (sp) -> t3-emit-const c n
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> t3-emit-text-lit c s
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-name c n ty depth
    is IrNegate (inner) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-negate c inner depth
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-binary c op l r ty depth
    is IrIf (cnd) (th) (el) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-if c cnd th el depth
    is IrLet (n) (ty) (v) (body) (sp) -> t3-emit-let c n v body depth
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-apply c e depth
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-act c stmts depth
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-record c fs ty depth
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-field-access c r f depth
    is IrMatch (s) (bs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-match c s bs depth
    is IrNumLit (n) (sp) -> t3-refuse c "real literal: IEEE 754 has no ternary form"
    is IrList (xs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-refuse c "list literal: a List is a growable value and this backend has a bump allocator with no reclamation"
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (ty) (sp) -> t3-refuse c ("field store: this backend builds records once and does not mutate them: ." & f)
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (ty) (sp) -> t3-refuse c "lambda"
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse c "unsupported IR form"

Section: Materialising a Constant

 TLIT carries a 13-trit immediate, which reaches only plus or minus 797,161
 against the word's 3,812,798,742,493. The encoding of an out-of-range
 immediate is a rem_euclid, so it WRAPS rather than failing, and an emitter
 that ignores the limit produces a plausible wrong number. Anything wider is
 built from balanced base-3^13 digits with TSHI, which multiplies by a power
 of three. Three digits cover the whole word range.

  t3-wide-max : Integer
  t3-wide-max = 797161

  t3-digit-base : Integer
  t3-digit-base = 1594323

  t3-bal-digit : Integer -> Integer
  t3-bal-digit (n) =
   let r = int-mod n t3-digit-base
   in if r > t3-wide-max then r - t3-digit-base else r

  t3-bal-rest : Integer -> Integer
  t3-bal-rest (n) = (n - t3-bal-digit n) / t3-digit-base

 This emits the immediate form, `TSHI Rd, Ra, #n`. The shift of 13 is
 exactly the 3-trit immediate's limit. Spec v1.3 resolves every
 three-address right-hand side as rhs = regs[r3] + imm, so the register
 form is equally legal and, re-measured 2026-08-10, equally correct; the
 immediate form is kept here because a shift amount known at compile time
 costs an instruction to put in a register and buys nothing.

 Measured 2026-08-09 against the previous emulator the register form
 answered 1 rather than 1594323, a silent multiply by 3^0 that did not
 fault. That behaviour changed in v1.3, and the guards this chapter and
 the encoder carried against it are gone. Recorded
 because it is the one place where trusting the reference over a
 measurement would have been right and the measurement was the thing that
 aged.

  t3-const-into : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-const-into (r) (n) =
   if n <= t3-wide-max then (if n >= 0 - t3-wide-max then t3-line ("TLIT  " & r & ", #" & show n) else t3-const-wide r n)
   else t3-const-wide r n

  t3-const-wide : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-const-wide (r) (n) =
   let d0 = t3-bal-digit n
   in let n1 = t3-bal-rest n
   in let d1 = t3-bal-digit n1
   in let n2 = t3-bal-rest n1
   in t3-line ("TLIT  " & r & ", #" & show n2)
    & t3-const-shift-add r d1
    & t3-const-shift-add r d0

  t3-const-shift-add : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-const-shift-add (r) (d) =
   t3-line ("TSHI  " & r & ", " & r & ", #13")
   & t3-line ("TLIT  " & t3-scratch-addr & ", #" & show d)
   & t3-line ("TADD  " & r & ", " & r & ", " & t3-scratch-addr)

  t3-emit-const : T3Ctx, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-const (c) (n) =
   let c2 = t3-push-delta c
   in T3Res { code = t3-const-into "R9" n & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

 A literal outside the word range is refused for the same reason a wide
 integer type is: the alternative is a saturated value that reads as data.

  t3-emit-int-lit : T3Ctx, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-int-lit (c) (n) =
   if n > t3-word-max then t3-refuse c ("integer literal exceeds the 27-trit word: " & show n)
   else if n < 0 - t3-word-max then t3-refuse c ("integer literal below the 27-trit word: " & show n)
   else t3-emit-const c n

 A text literal becomes a .data entry. Its address is resolved by the
 assembler, so the emitter only has to name it consistently.

  t3-emit-text-lit : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-text-lit (c) (s) =
   let idx = list-length (c.strings)
   in let c2 = t3-add-string c s
   in let c3 = t3-push-delta c2
   in T3Res { code = t3-line ("TLIT  R9, #str" & show idx) & t3-push "R9", ctx = c3 }

  t3-emit-name : T3Ctx, Text, CodexType, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-name (c) (n) (ty) (depth) =
   let slot = t3-slot-of (c.env) n
   in if slot >= 0 then t3-emit-local c slot
   else if n == "True" then t3-emit-const c 1
   else if n == "False" then t3-emit-const c 0
   else if t3-arity-of (c.arities) n == 0 then t3-emit-call c n 0 ""
   else if t3-ctor-index (c.type-defs) ty n >= 0 then t3-emit-ctor c n ty (t3-ctor-index (c.type-defs) ty n) [] depth
   else t3-refuse c ("name not in scope, not a nullary function and not a constructor: " & n)

  t3-emit-local : T3Ctx, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-local (c) (slot) =
   let ld = t3-load-slot c "R9" slot
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c
   in T3Res { code = ld & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

  t3-emit-negate : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-negate (c) (inner) (depth) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c inner (depth + 1)
   in let c2 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let c3 = t3-push-delta c2
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R9" & t3-line "TNEG  R9, R9" & t3-push "R9", ctx = c3 }

Section: Binary Emission

 Left then right, both on the stack, then popped in reverse so the right
 operand lands in the scratch B register and the left in scratch A. Doing
 it through the stack rather than through registers is what makes
 arbitrary nesting correct on a machine where every register is
 caller-save and a nested call would otherwise clobber a held operand.

  t3-emit-binary : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-binary (c) (op) (l) (r) (ty) (depth) =
   if t3-ov-supported ty == False then t3-refuse c "wrapping overflow has no ternary meaning"
   else if t3-int-ty-fits ty == False then t3-refuse c "integer band wider than a 27-trit word"
   else t3-emit-binary-go c op l r depth

  t3-emit-binary-go : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-binary-go (c) (op) (l) (r) (depth) =
   let ls = t3-emit-expr-at c l (depth + 1)
   in let rs = t3-emit-expr-at (ls.ctx) r (depth + 1)
   in let popped = t3-pop-delta (t3-pop-delta (rs.ctx))
   in let pre = ls.code & rs.code & t3-pop t3-scratch-b & t3-pop t3-scratch-a
   in t3-emit-binary-op popped op pre

  t3-emit-binary-op : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-binary-op (c) (op) (pre) =
   if t3-is-comparison op then t3-emit-compare c op pre
   else t3-emit-arith c op pre

  t3-emit-arith : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-arith (c) (op) (pre) =
   let m = t3-arith-mnemonic op
   in if m == "" then t3-emit-arith-other c op pre
   else let c2 = t3-push-delta c
   in T3Res { code = pre & t3-line (m & "  R9, R9, R10") & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

 Text append as a VALUE has no form here. fmt::concat is declared in their
 stdlib but is not among the 9 of 31 fmt natives the T3 backend implements
 (spec v1.3, section 8), so a program using it fails at ASSEMBLE time with
 an undefined label rather than at run time. Earlier revisions listed it at
 syscall 142, where no handler has ever existed. Strings are not
 addressable either, so there is nothing to build a joined string in.
 Append survives only where it is being printed, which t3-print-parts
 linearises into one print per leaf.

  t3-emit-arith-other : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-arith-other (c) (op) (pre) =
   when op
    is IrAppendText -> t3-refuse c "text append outside a print: fmt::concat is not among the fmt natives the T3 backend implements, so it fails at assemble time, and strings are not addressable"
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse c (t3-other-op-reason op)

 A real operation is named as such rather than lumped in with the rest,
 because it is the sharpest of the failures predicted in advance and the
 whole point is to report WHICH assumption the IR carried, not that
 something went wrong.

  t3-other-op-reason : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  t3-other-op-reason (op) =
   if t3-is-real-op op then "real arithmetic: IEEE 754 is a binary format by construction and has no ternary form"
   else if t3-is-vec-op op then "vector arithmetic: T3ISA has no SIMD"
   else "binary operator with no T3ISA form"

  t3-is-real-op : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  t3-is-real-op (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddNum -> True
    is IrSubNum -> True
    is IrMulNum -> True
    is IrDivNum -> True
    is IrAddRealApprox -> True
    is IrSubRealApprox -> True
    is IrMulRealApprox -> True
    is IrDivRealApprox -> True
    is IrAddRealTrapping -> True
    is IrSubRealTrapping -> True
    is IrMulRealTrapping -> True
    is IrDivRealTrapping -> True
    is IrAddRealSaturating -> True
    is IrSubRealSaturating -> True
    is IrMulRealSaturating -> True
    is IrDivRealSaturating -> True
    is IrApproxEq -> True
    is IrApproxEqExact -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  t3-is-vec-op : IRBinaryOp -> Boolean
  t3-is-vec-op (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddVec -> True
    is IrSubVec -> True
    is IrMulVec -> True
    is IrDivVec -> True
    is IrLtVec -> True
    is IrGtVec -> True
    is IrLtEqVec -> True
    is IrGtEqVec -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  t3-emit-compare : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-compare (c) (op) (pre) =
   let c2 = t3-push-delta c
   in let cmp = t3-line "TCMP  R9, R9, R10"
   in T3Res { code = pre & cmp & t3-cmp-recover op & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

Section: Control Flow

 TBRANCH reads the register directly rather than FLAGS and dispatches on
 its sign, so a boolean condition takes the positive arm at one and the
 zero arm at zero. The negative arm goes to the else label too: a Codex
 boolean is never negative, and sending it anywhere else would turn a
 corrupt value into a wild jump.

  t3-emit-if : T3Ctx, IRExpr, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-if (c) (cnd) (th) (el) (depth) =
   let cs = t3-emit-expr-at c cnd (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (cs.ctx)
   in let lbl = t3-fresh-label c1
   in let c2 = t3-bump-label c1
   in t3-emit-if-arms c2 (cs.code) lbl th el depth

  t3-emit-if-arms : T3Ctx, Text, Text, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-if-arms (c) (cond-code) (lbl) (th) (el) (depth) =
   let ts = t3-emit-expr-at c th (depth + 1)
   in let c-else = t3-pop-delta (ts.ctx)
   in let es = t3-emit-expr-at c-else el (depth + 1)
   in T3Res { code = t3-if-code cond-code lbl (ts.code) (es.code), ctx = es.ctx }

 Both arms leave one word, so the merge point is balanced and sp-delta
 after the whole form is the same whichever arm ran.

  t3-if-code : Text, Text, Text, Text -> Text
  t3-if-code (cond-code) (lbl) (then-code) (else-code) =
   cond-code
   & t3-pop "R9"
   & t3-line ("TBRANCH R9, " & lbl & "_then, " & lbl & "_else, " & lbl & "_else")
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_then")
   & then-code
   & t3-line ("JUMP  " & lbl & "_end")
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_else")
   & else-code
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_end")

 A let evaluates its value, stores it into a fresh frame slot, and then
 emits the body with the name bound. The slot is never reused, so the
 frame pre-pass counting lets is what sizes the frame.

  t3-emit-let : T3Ctx, Text, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-let (c) (n) (v) (body) (depth) =
   let vs = t3-emit-expr-at c v (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (vs.ctx)
   in let c2 = t3-bind-slot c1 n
   in let slot = t3-slot-of (c2.env) n
   in let store = t3-pop "R9" & t3-store-slot c1 "R9" slot
   in let bs = t3-emit-expr-at c2 body (depth + 1)
   in T3Res { code = vs.code & store & bs.code, ctx = bs.ctx }

Section: Match

 The scrutinee is evaluated once into a frame slot, because every branch has
 to read it and the branches run at different stack depths. Each branch is a
 test that jumps to the NEXT branch's label on failure, then its bindings,
 then its guard, then its body, then a jump to the end. Branch i's failure
 label is branch i+1's entry, so the chain needs no patching pass, which is
 what makes a single forward walk enough on a machine whose assembler resolves
 labels for us.

 A match that falls off the end traps. The type checker proves exhaustiveness
 before the IR is written, so this is unreachable in a well-formed program;
 emitting nothing there would leave the stack short by one word and every
 offset above it wrong, which is a corrupted answer rather than a failure.

  t3-emit-match : T3Ctx, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-match (c) (scrut) (bs) (depth) =
   let ss = t3-emit-expr-at c scrut (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (ss.ctx)
   in let mlbl = t3-fresh-label c1
   in let c2 = t3-bind-slot (t3-bump-label c1) (mlbl & "_s")
   in let slot = t3-slot-of (c2.env) (mlbl & "_s")
   in let store = t3-pop "R9" & t3-store-slot c1 "R9" slot
   in let br = t3-emit-branches c2 bs slot mlbl 0 depth
   in T3Res { code = ss.code & store & br.code, ctx = t3-push-delta (br.ctx) }

 Each branch is emitted from the SAME stack depth, so the depth is wound back
 between them rather than accumulating: only one branch runs.

  t3-emit-branches : T3Ctx, List IRBranch, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-branches (c) (bs) (slot) (mlbl) (i) (depth) =
   if i >= list-length bs then T3Res { code = t3-match-fail mlbl i, ctx = c }
   else let one = t3-emit-branch c (list-at bs i) slot mlbl i depth
   in let rest = t3-emit-branches (t3-pop-delta (one.ctx)) bs slot mlbl (i + 1) depth
   in T3Res { code = one.code & rest.code, ctx = rest.ctx }

  t3-match-fail : Text, Integer -> Text
  t3-match-fail (mlbl) (i) =
   t3-label-line (mlbl & "_b" & show i)
   & t3-line "TLIT  R1, #1"
   & t3-line "TDIV  R1, R1, R0"
   & t3-label-line (mlbl & "_end")

  t3-emit-branch : T3Ctx, IRBranch, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-branch (c) (br) (slot) (mlbl) (i) (depth) =
   let next = mlbl & "_b" & show (i + 1)
   in let t = t3-emit-pattern c (br.pattern) slot next mlbl i
   in let g = t3-emit-guard (t.ctx) (br.guard) next mlbl i depth
   in let b = t3-emit-expr-at (g.ctx) (br.body) (depth + 1)
   in let head = t3-label-line (mlbl & "_b" & show i)
   in T3Res { code = head & t.code & g.code & b.code & t3-line ("JUMP  " & mlbl & "_end"), ctx = b.ctx }

 A guard that is the literal True is the shape every unguarded branch parses
 to, and it emits nothing.

  t3-emit-guard : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-guard (c) (g) (next) (mlbl) (i) (depth) =
   when g
    is IrBoolLit (v) (sp) -> if v then T3Res { code = "", ctx = c } else T3Res { code = t3-line ("JUMP  " & next), ctx = c }
    is otherwise -> t3-emit-guard-test c g next mlbl i depth

  t3-emit-guard-test : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-guard-test (c) (g) (next) (mlbl) (i) (depth) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c g (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let ok = mlbl & "_g" & show i
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R9" & t3-line ("TBRANCH R9, " & ok & ", " & next & ", " & next) & t3-label-line ok, ctx = c1 }

Section: Patterns

 A pattern emits its test and its bindings together, because a constructor
 pattern's bindings are only valid once its tag has matched.

  t3-emit-pattern : T3Ctx, IRPat, Integer, Text, Text, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-pattern (c) (p) (slot) (next) (mlbl) (i) =
   when p
    is IrWildPat (sp) -> T3Res { code = "", ctx = c }
    is IrVarPat (n) (ty) (sp) -> t3-bind-scrutinee c n slot
    is IrLitPat (v) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-lit-pattern c v slot next
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-emit-ctor-pattern c n subs ty slot next
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse-void c "pattern form with no T3ISA lowering"

  t3-bind-scrutinee : T3Ctx, Text, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-bind-scrutinee (c) (n) (slot) =
   let c1 = t3-bind-slot c n
   in let dst = t3-slot-of (c1.env) n
   in T3Res { code = t3-load-slot c "R9" slot & t3-store-slot c "R9" dst, ctx = c1 }

 A boolean pattern spells its value as a name, which is why the two are
 written out here rather than parsed as numbers.

  t3-pat-lit-value : Text -> Integer
  t3-pat-lit-value (t) =
   if t == "True" then 1
   else if t == "False" then 0
   else text-to-integer t

  t3-emit-lit-pattern : T3Ctx, Text, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-lit-pattern (c) (v) (slot) (next) =
   let ld = t3-load-slot c "R9" slot
   in let cmp = t3-const-into "R10" (t3-pat-lit-value v) & t3-line "TCMP  R9, R9, R10"
   in T3Res { code = ld & cmp & t3-line ("TBRANCH R9, " & next & ", " & t3-lit-ok next & ", " & next) & t3-label-line (t3-lit-ok next), ctx = c }

 The equal case is the ZERO arm of the three-way branch, so the label it needs
 is derived from the failure label rather than taking another counter.

  t3-lit-ok : Text -> Text
  t3-lit-ok (next) = next & "_eq"

 The tag is at offset zero and the constructor's fields follow it. Only a name
 or a wildcard is accepted underneath, because a nested pattern would need a
 test tree this walk does not build, and matching it loosely would accept
 values the source rejects.

  t3-emit-ctor-pattern : T3Ctx, Text, List IRPat, CodexType, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-ctor-pattern (c) (n) (subs) (ty) (slot) (next) =
   let tag = t3-ctor-index (c.type-defs) ty n
   in if tag < 0 then t3-refuse-void c ("constructor pattern whose type is not recoverable: " & n)
   else if t3-subs-simple subs 0 == False then t3-refuse-void c ("nested pattern under constructor " & n & ": only a name or a wildcard is accepted")
   else t3-emit-ctor-pattern-go c subs tag slot next

  t3-subs-simple : List IRPat, Integer -> Boolean
  t3-subs-simple (subs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length subs then True
   else when list-at subs i
    is IrVarPat (n) (ty) (sp) -> t3-subs-simple subs (i + 1)
    is IrWildPat (sp) -> t3-subs-simple subs (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> False

  t3-emit-ctor-pattern-go : T3Ctx, List IRPat, Integer, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-ctor-pattern-go (c) (subs) (tag) (slot) (next) =
   let ok = next & "_tag"
   in let ld = t3-load-slot c "R22" slot
   in let test = t3-load-obj "R23" 0
    & t3-const-into "R24" tag
    & t3-line "TCMP  R23, R23, R24"
    & t3-line ("TBRANCH R23, " & next & ", " & ok & ", " & next)
    & t3-label-line ok
   in let b = t3-bind-ctor-fields c subs 0 ""
   in T3Res { code = ld & test & b.code, ctx = b.ctx }

 R22 still holds the scrutinee here, which is why the binds read through
 t3-load-obj and nothing between the tag test and the last bind may write it.

  t3-bind-ctor-fields : T3Ctx, List IRPat, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-bind-ctor-fields (c) (subs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length subs then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else when list-at subs i
    is IrVarPat (n) (ty) (sp) ->
     let c1 = t3-bind-slot c n
     in let dst = t3-slot-of (c1.env) n
     in t3-bind-ctor-fields c1 subs (i + 1) (acc & t3-load-obj "R9" (i + 1) & t3-store-slot c "R9" dst)
    is otherwise -> t3-bind-ctor-fields c subs (i + 1) acc

Section: Application

 An application is flattened to a root and its arguments. Only a direct
 call of a known definition, a recognised console or format builtin, is
 supported; a call through a value would need an indirect CALLR and a
 closure representation this backend does not have.

  t3-emit-apply : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-apply (c) (e) (depth) =
   let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
   in when chain.root
    is IrName (n) (nty) (sp) -> t3-emit-known-call c n (chain.args) depth (t3-apply-type e) nty
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse c "call through a computed function value"

  t3-apply-type : IRExpr -> CodexType
  t3-apply-type (e) =
   when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> ty
    is otherwise -> ErrorTy

  t3-emit-known-call : T3Ctx, Text, List IRExpr, Integer, CodexType, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-known-call (c) (n) (args) (depth) (rty) (fty) =
   if n == "show" then t3-refuse c "show outside a print: fmt::show_int at syscall 14 would build the handle, but this backend has no Text value to put it in"
   else if n == "print-line-uni" then t3-emit-print c args depth
   else if n == "int-rem" then t3-emit-builtin2 c args depth t3-rem-body
   else if n == "int-mod" then t3-emit-builtin2 c args depth t3-mod-body
   else if n == "__narrow" then t3-emit-narrow c args depth rty
   else if t3-ctor-index (c.type-defs) rty n >= 0 then t3-emit-ctor c n rty (t3-ctor-index (c.type-defs) rty n) args depth
   else t3-emit-user-call c n args depth fty

Section: Checked Narrow

 __narrow asserts at runtime that a value lies in a declared band. THE NODE
 DOES NOT CARRY THE BAND: it is typed

   (apply (name "__narrow" (fn int-default int-default)) <arg> int-default)

 default to default, whatever band was asserted. The band is in the IR, but
 it is held by the ENCLOSING CONTEXT, and which context depends on where the
 narrow sits. Measured 2026-08-09 across three positions: at a def's return
 it is the def's own type `(fn int-default (int 0 100 ov-error))`; at a call
 argument it is the callee's parameter type
 `(name "takes-small" (fn (int 0 100 ov-error) ...))`; at a record field it
 is the field's declaration in the type-defs.

 So the check IS emittable, and this emitter takes the band from the
 position rather than from the node. A narrow anywhere else is refused,
 because a weaker check that looks like the real one is the failure this
 backend exists to avoid: emitting a word-range test where the source asked
 for `0 and 100` would pass two million and read as an enforced bound.

 This paragraph described the band as ABSENT until it was measured properly.
 It was not absent; the instrument was a look at the node, which cannot show
 a band the context holds. The lesson is L-ERASED's inverse and worth as
 much: before reporting that an artifact has lost something, look at what
 encloses it.

 The trap is a deliberate division by zero, which is the only fault this
 machine is known to raise. The published reference says division by zero
 returns 0 as emulator-defined behaviour and not a fault; MEASURED, the
 emulator prints "TRAP: division by zero" and stops. The reference is wrong
 about it and the measured behaviour is the one relied on here.

  t3-is-narrow-apply : IRExpr -> Boolean
  t3-is-narrow-apply (e) =
   when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> t3-root-is-narrow f
    is otherwise -> False

  t3-root-is-narrow : IRExpr -> Boolean
  t3-root-is-narrow (f) =
   when f
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> n == "__narrow"
    is otherwise -> False

 Emit a subexpression whose expected type is known from its position. Only
 __narrow cares; everything else ignores the band and goes the usual way.

  t3-emit-expr-expecting : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-expr-expecting (c) (e) (depth) (expected) =
   if t3-is-narrow-apply e then t3-emit-narrow-banded c e depth expected
   else t3-emit-expr-at c e depth

  t3-emit-narrow-banded : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-narrow-banded (c) (e) (depth) (expected) =
   let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
   in if list-length (chain.args) /= 1 then t3-refuse c "__narrow takes one argument"
   else when expected
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (ov) -> t3-emit-narrow-checked c (chain.args) depth lo hi
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse c "__narrow where the declared band is not recoverable from the position"

  t3-emit-narrow-checked : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-narrow-checked (c) (args) (depth) (lo) (hi) =
   if hi > t3-word-max then t3-refuse c "__narrow band wider than a 27-trit word"
   else if lo < 0 - t3-word-max then t3-refuse c "__narrow band wider than a 27-trit word"
   else t3-emit-narrow-code c args depth lo hi

 Reached only when a narrow turns up somewhere the band cannot be read off
 the position. Refused rather than approximated.

  t3-emit-narrow : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-narrow (c) (args) (depth) (rty) =
   t3-refuse c "__narrow where the declared band is not recoverable from the position"

  t3-fn-param-type : CodexType, Integer -> CodexType
  t3-fn-param-type (ty) (i) =
   when ty
    is FunTy (p) (row) (r) -> if i == 0 then p else t3-fn-param-type r (i - 1)
    is ForAllTy (id) (b) -> t3-fn-param-type b i
    is EffectfulTy (es) (ss) (inner) -> t3-fn-param-type inner i
    is otherwise -> ErrorTy

  t3-emit-narrow-code : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-narrow-code (c) (args) (depth) (lo) (hi) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c (list-at args 0) (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let lbl = t3-fresh-label c1
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta (t3-bump-label c1)
   in let note = t3-line ("; __narrow: assert " & show lo & " <= v <= " & show hi)
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R9" & note & t3-narrow-check lo hi lbl & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

 Two comparisons folded into one sign: minus one if below the floor, minus
 one if above the ceiling, so their sum is negative exactly when the value
 is out of band and the three-way branch needs no second test.

  t3-narrow-check : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-narrow-check (lo) (hi) (lbl) =
   t3-const-into "R11" lo
   & t3-line "TCMP  R12, R9, R11"
   & t3-line "TMIN  R12, R12, R0"
   & t3-const-into "R11" hi
   & t3-line "TCMP  R13, R11, R9"
   & t3-line "TMIN  R13, R13, R0"
   & t3-line "TADD  R12, R12, R13"
   & t3-line ("TBRANCH R12, " & lbl & "_nok, " & lbl & "_nok, " & lbl & "_nbad")
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_nbad")
   & t3-line "TLIT  R1, #1"
   & t3-line "TDIV  R1, R1, R0"
   & t3-label-line (lbl & "_nok")

 TMOD is the TRUNCATING remainder, measured against the oracle on all three
 sign combinations: -7 mod 3 is -1, 7 mod -3 is 1, -7 mod -3 is -1. That is
 Codex int-rem exactly, and it is NOT int-mod. The published reference says
 only "Rd = Ra mod Rb", which does not distinguish them, and choosing wrong
 here is silent for positive operands and wrong for negative ones.

  t3-rem-body : Text
  t3-rem-body = t3-line "TMOD  R9, R9, R10"

 The Euclidean remainder is the truncating one plus the divisor's magnitude
 when it came out negative. Absolute value is max(b, -b); the flag is the
 negated min of the sign with zero, which is one exactly when the remainder
 is below zero.

  t3-mod-body : Text
  t3-mod-body =
   t3-line "TMOD  R11, R9, R10"
   & t3-line "TNEG  R12, R10"
   & t3-line "TMAX  R12, R10, R12"
   & t3-line "TCMP  R13, R11, R0"
   & t3-line "TMIN  R13, R13, R0"
   & t3-line "TNEG  R13, R13"
   & t3-line "TMUL  R13, R13, R12"
   & t3-line "TADD  R9, R11, R13"

  t3-emit-builtin2 : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-builtin2 (c) (args) (depth) (body) =
   if list-length args /= 2 then t3-refuse c "builtin takes two arguments"
   else t3-emit-builtin2-go c args depth body

  t3-emit-builtin2-go : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-builtin2-go (c) (args) (depth) (body) =
   let ls = t3-emit-expr-at c (list-at args 0) (depth + 1)
   in let rs = t3-emit-expr-at (ls.ctx) (list-at args 1) (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (t3-pop-delta (rs.ctx))
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c1
   in let pre = ls.code & rs.code & t3-pop t3-scratch-b & t3-pop t3-scratch-a
   in T3Res { code = pre & body & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

  t3-syscall-print-int : Text
  t3-syscall-print-int = t3-line "SYSCALL #1"

 THERE ARE NO FIRST-CLASS TEXT VALUES ON THIS BACKEND. Strings are not in
 addressable memory; a literal is an entry in a sidecar table and its
 handle is an index into it.

 The reason used to be that the machine could not build one. Earlier
 revisions of the reference put fmt::show_int at 140 and fmt::concat at
 142, and measured 2026-08-09, the emulator answered "TRAP: unknown
 syscall" for both, because no handler has ever existed at those numbers.
 Spec v1.3 gives the real ones. Re-measured 2026-08-10: syscall 14 with 42
 in R1, then syscall 3, prints "42", so show_int does work and does hand
 back a printable handle. fmt::concat is a different case and still cannot
 be reached at all, being one of the 22 of 31 fmt natives the T3 backend
 does not implement.

 So `show` is now a backend choice rather than a machine limit: what is
 missing is a Text representation in this emitter to carry the handle,
 not a way to make one. Append remains impossible either way.

 What CAN be done is emit a line. A print of an append tree linearises into
 one syscall per leaf: a literal prints with print_str, an integer with
 print_int, and the newline comes once at the end. That is the general
 lowering of formatted output for a target with no string heap, not a
 special case of any one program, and it is why print-line-uni is handled
 by walking its argument rather than by evaluating it.

  t3-emit-print : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-print (c) (args) (depth) =
   if list-length args /= 1 then t3-refuse c "print-line-uni takes one argument"
   else let p = t3-print-parts c (list-at args 0) depth
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta (p.ctx)
   in T3Res { code = p.code & t3-line "SYSCALL #4" & t3-line "TLIT  R9, #0" & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

 Each part emits its own print and leaves the stack as it found it.

  t3-print-parts : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-print-parts (c) (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= 256 then t3-refuse-void c "print nesting beyond 256"
   else when e
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> t3-print-append c op l r depth
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> t3-print-literal c s
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> t3-print-apply c e depth
    is otherwise -> t3-print-int-expr c e depth

  t3-print-append : T3Ctx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-print-append (c) (op) (l) (r) (depth) =
   when op
    is IrAppendText ->
     let ls = t3-print-parts c l (depth + 1)
     in let rs = t3-print-parts (ls.ctx) r (depth + 1)
     in T3Res { code = ls.code & rs.code, ctx = rs.ctx }
    is otherwise -> t3-print-int-expr c (IrBinary op l r ErrorTy synthetic-span) depth

  t3-print-literal : T3Ctx, Text -> T3Res
  t3-print-literal (c) (s) =
   let idx = list-length (c.strings)
   in let c2 = t3-add-string c s
   in T3Res { code = t3-line ("TLIT  R1, #str" & show idx) & t3-line "SYSCALL #3", ctx = c2 }

 A `show` under a print is the integer path: print_int renders it directly,
 which is the same rendering fmt::show_int would have produced had it
 existed.

  t3-print-apply : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-print-apply (c) (e) (depth) =
   let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
   in when chain.root
    is IrName (n) (nty) (sp) -> t3-print-named c n (chain.args) e depth
    is otherwise -> t3-print-int-expr c e depth

  t3-print-named : T3Ctx, Text, List IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-print-named (c) (n) (args) (e) (depth) =
   if n == "show" then (if list-length args == 1 then t3-print-int-expr c (list-at args 0) depth
                        else t3-refuse-void c "show takes one argument")
   else t3-print-int-expr c e depth

  t3-print-int-expr : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-print-int-expr (c) (e) (depth) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c e (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R1" & t3-line "SYSCALL #1", ctx = c1 }

  t3-emit-builtin1 : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-builtin1 (c) (args) (depth) (call) =
   if list-length args /= 1 then t3-refuse c "builtin takes one argument"
   else let s = t3-emit-expr-at c (list-at args 0) (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c1
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R1" & call & t3-push "R1", ctx = c2 }

  t3-emit-builtin1-void : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-builtin1-void (c) (args) (depth) (call) =
   if list-length args /= 1 then t3-refuse c "builtin takes one argument"
   else let s = t3-emit-expr-at c (list-at args 0) (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c1
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-pop "R1" & call & t3-line "TLIT  R9, #0" & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

 Arguments are evaluated left to right onto the stack and only then popped
 into R1 upward. Because the last one pushed is the last argument, the
 pops run from the highest register down.

  t3-emit-user-call : T3Ctx, Text, List IRExpr, Integer, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-user-call (c) (n) (args) (depth) (fty) =
   let want = t3-arity-of (c.arities) n
   in if want < 0 then t3-refuse c ("call to an unknown function: " & n)
   else if want /= list-length args then t3-refuse c ("partial or over-applied call: " & n)
   else if list-length args > t3-max-args then t3-refuse c ("more than eight arguments: " & n)
   else t3-emit-call-args c n args depth fty

  t3-emit-call-args : T3Ctx, Text, List IRExpr, Integer, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-call-args (c) (n) (args) (depth) (fty) =
   let ev = t3-emit-args c args 0 depth "" fty
   in t3-emit-call (ev.ctx) n (list-length args) (ev.code)

 Each argument is emitted knowing the parameter type it is going into, which
 is where a __narrow in argument position finds its band.

  t3-emit-args : T3Ctx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Text, CodexType -> T3Res
  t3-emit-args (c) (args) (i) (depth) (acc) (fty) =
   if i >= list-length args then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else let s = t3-emit-expr-expecting c (list-at args i) (depth + 1) (t3-fn-param-type fty i)
   in t3-emit-args (s.ctx) args (i + 1) depth (acc & s.code) fty

  t3-emit-call : T3Ctx, Text, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-call (c) (n) (argc) (pre) =
   let c1 = t3-drop-deltas c argc
   in let c2 = t3-push-delta c1
   in let body = pre & t3-pop-args argc & t3-line ("CALL  " & t3-sanitize n) & t3-push "R1"
   in T3Res { code = body, ctx = c2 }

  t3-drop-deltas : T3Ctx, Integer -> T3Ctx
  t3-drop-deltas (c) (k) =
   if k <= 0 then c else t3-drop-deltas (t3-pop-delta c) (k - 1)

  t3-pop-args : Integer -> Text
  t3-pop-args (argc) = t3-pop-args-loop argc ""

  t3-pop-args-loop : Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-pop-args-loop (i) (acc) =
   if i <= 0 then acc
   else t3-pop-args-loop (i - 1) (acc & t3-pop ("R" & show i))

Section: Act Blocks

 An act block runs its statements in order and answers the value of the
 last one. A bound name gets a frame slot exactly as a let does.

  t3-emit-act : T3Ctx, List IRActStmt, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act (c) (stmts) (depth) =
   if list-length stmts == 0 then t3-emit-const c 0
   else t3-emit-act-loop c stmts 0 depth ""

  t3-emit-act-loop : T3Ctx, List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act-loop (c) (stmts) (i) (depth) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length stmts then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else let last = i == list-length stmts - 1
   in let s = t3-emit-act-stmt c (list-at stmts i) depth last
   in t3-emit-act-loop (s.ctx) stmts (i + 1) depth (acc & s.code)

 Every statement but the last has its value dropped, which keeps the stack
 balanced across a block of any length.

  t3-emit-act-stmt : T3Ctx, IRActStmt, Integer, Boolean -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act-stmt (c) (st) (depth) (last) =
   when st
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> t3-emit-act-exec c e depth last
    is IrDoBind (n) (ty) (e) (sp) -> t3-emit-act-bind c n e depth last
    is otherwise -> t3-refuse c "unsupported act statement"

  t3-emit-act-exec : T3Ctx, IRExpr, Integer, Boolean -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act-exec (c) (e) (depth) (last) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c e (depth + 1)
   in if last then s
   else let c2 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in T3Res { code = s.code & t3-sp-adjust "TADD" 1, ctx = c2 }

  t3-emit-act-bind : T3Ctx, Text, IRExpr, Integer, Boolean -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act-bind (c) (n) (e) (depth) (last) =
   let s = t3-emit-expr-at c e (depth + 1)
   in let c1 = t3-pop-delta (s.ctx)
   in let c2 = t3-bind-slot c1 n
   in let slot = t3-slot-of (c2.env) n
   in let store = t3-pop "R9" & t3-store-slot c1 "R9" slot
   in t3-emit-act-bind-tail c2 (s.code & store) slot last

  t3-emit-act-bind-tail : T3Ctx, Text, Integer, Boolean -> T3Res
  t3-emit-act-bind-tail (c) (pre) (slot) (last) =
   if last == False then T3Res { code = pre, ctx = c }
   else let c2 = t3-push-delta c
   in T3Res { code = pre & t3-load-slot c "R9" slot & t3-push "R9", ctx = c2 }

Section: Frame Sizing

 A let never reuses a slot, so the frame is the parameter count plus every
 binder in the body. Counting them up front is what lets the prologue
 allocate once and the body never move the stack pointer except to push.

  t3-count-binders : IRExpr -> Integer
  t3-count-binders (e) =
   when e
    is IrLet (n) (ty) (v) (b) (sp) -> 1 + t3-count-binders v + t3-count-binders b
    is IrIf (cnd) (th) (el) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-binders cnd + t3-count-binders th + t3-count-binders el
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-binders l + t3-count-binders r
    is IrNegate (inner) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-binders inner
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-binders f + t3-count-binders a
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-stmt-binders stmts 0 0
    is IrMatch (s) (bs) (ty) (sp) -> 1 + t3-count-binders s + t3-count-branch-binders bs 0 0
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-field-binders fs 0 0
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-binders r
    is otherwise -> 0

 A match adds a slot for the scrutinee, and every branch adds one per name its
 pattern binds. A frame short by one is not a crash: it is every local above
 the shortfall reading the wrong word.

  t3-count-branch-binders : List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  t3-count-branch-binders (bs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length bs then acc
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let n = t3-count-pat-binders (b.pattern) + t3-count-binders (b.body) + t3-count-binders (b.guard)
   in t3-count-branch-binders bs (i + 1) (acc + n)

  t3-count-pat-binders : IRPat -> Integer
  t3-count-pat-binders (p) =
   when p
    is IrVarPat (n) (ty) (sp) -> 1
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> t3-count-sub-binders subs 0 0
    is otherwise -> 0

  t3-count-sub-binders : List IRPat, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  t3-count-sub-binders (subs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length subs then acc
   else t3-count-sub-binders subs (i + 1) (acc + t3-count-pat-binders (list-at subs i))

  t3-count-field-binders : List IRFieldVal, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  t3-count-field-binders (fs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length fs then acc
   else t3-count-field-binders fs (i + 1) (acc + t3-count-binders ((list-at fs i).value))

  t3-count-stmt-binders : List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  t3-count-stmt-binders (stmts) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length stmts then acc
   else t3-count-stmt-binders stmts (i + 1) (acc + t3-count-one-stmt (list-at stmts i))

  t3-count-one-stmt : IRActStmt -> Integer
  t3-count-one-stmt (st) =
   when st
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> t3-count-binders e
    is IrDoBind (n) (ty) (e) (sp) -> 1 + t3-count-binders e
    is otherwise -> 0

Section: Definition Emission

  t3-emit-def : T3Ctx, IRDef -> T3Res
  t3-emit-def (c) (d) =
   let np = list-length (d.params)
   in if np > t3-max-args then t3-refuse c ("more than eight parameters: " & d.name)
   else t3-emit-def-go c d np

  t3-emit-def-go : T3Ctx, IRDef, Integer -> T3Res
  t3-emit-def-go (c) (d) (np) =
   let tag = t3-sanitize (d.name)
   in let c0 = t3-reset-fn c tag
   in let c1 = t3-bind-params c0 (d.params) 0
   in let frame = np + t3-count-binders (d.body)
   in let bs = t3-emit-expr-expecting c1 (d.body) 0 (strip-fun-args (d.type-val))
   in T3Res { code = t3-def-code tag frame np (bs.code), ctx = bs.ctx }

  t3-bind-params : T3Ctx, List IRParam, Integer -> T3Ctx
  t3-bind-params (c) (ps) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then c
   else let p = list-at ps i
   in t3-bind-params (t3-bind-slot c (p.name)) ps (i + 1)

 The prologue allocates the whole frame and spills the incoming argument
 registers into their slots, because every register is caller-save and the
 first nested call would otherwise lose them.

  t3-def-code : Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-def-code (tag) (frame) (np) (body) =
   t3-label-line tag
   & t3-sp-adjust "TSUB" frame
   & t3-spill-params np 0 ""
   & body
   & t3-pop "R1"
   & t3-sp-adjust "TADD" frame
   & t3-line "RET"

  t3-spill-params : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-spill-params (np) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= np then acc
   else t3-spill-params np (i + 1) (acc & t3-line ("STORE R" & show (i + 1) & ", [R26+#" & show i & "]"))

Section: Chapter Emission

  t3-emit-defs : T3Ctx, List IRDef, Integer, Text -> T3Res
  t3-emit-defs (c) (defs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length defs then T3Res { code = acc, ctx = c }
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in let s = t3-emit-def c d
   in t3-emit-defs (s.ctx) defs (i + 1) (acc & "\n" & s.code)

  t3-build-arities : List IRDef, Integer, List T3Arity -> List T3Arity
  t3-build-arities (defs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length defs then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in let a = T3Arity { fn-name = d.name, fn-arity = list-length (d.params) }
   in t3-build-arities defs (i + 1) (list-push acc a)

 The program starts by jumping to opening, this language's entry point,
 and ends when that function returns: the emulator halts on a RET with an
 empty call stack, so no HALT is needed, which is fortunate because their
 front end never emits one and its opcode has never been measured.

  emit-t3isa-chapter : IRChapter, List ATypeDef -> Text
  emit-t3isa-chapter (chap) (tds) =
   let ars = t3-build-arities (chap.defs) 0 []
   in let c0 = t3-empty-ctx ars tds
   in let r = t3-emit-defs c0 (chap.defs) 0 ""
   in t3-assemble-output (r.ctx) (r.code)

  t3-assemble-output : T3Ctx, Text -> Text
  t3-assemble-output (c) (body) =
   t3-header & t3-heap-init & t3-line "JUMP  opening" & body & "\n" & t3-data-section (c.strings)

  t3-header : Text
  t3-header =
   "; T3ISA assembly, emitted by the Codex t3isa plug\n; 27-trit balanced ternary word machine\n\n"

  t3-data-section : List Text -> Text
  t3-data-section (ss) =
   if list-length ss == 0 then "" else ".data:\n" & t3-data-lines ss 0 ""

  t3-data-lines : List Text, Integer, Text -> Text
  t3-data-lines (ss) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length ss then acc
   else t3-data-lines ss (i + 1) (acc & "  str" & show i & ": .string \"" & t3-escape (list-at ss i) & "\"\n")

 A quote inside a literal must be escaped for the assembler to find the
 closing delimiter, and a backslash must be escaped so it is not read as
 the start of one.

  t3-escape : Text -> Text
  t3-escape (s) = text-replace (text-replace s "\\" "\\\\") "\"" "\\\""
